# Line-ending policy for a team working across several computers.
#
# Why this file is necessary: git's core.autocrlf setting lives in per-machine config, which does
# NOT travel with a clone. So without this file, whether your checkout gets LF or CRLF depends on
# how each student's git happened to be installed. That produces diffs where the only change is
# invisible whitespace, and it makes merges harder than they need to be.
#
# Normalise everything to LF in the repository. Git still checks text files out as CRLF on Windows
# where that is the platform default, so nothing changes for you day to day.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must stay CRLF or cmd.exe mis-parses them.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# gradlew is a shell script. It MUST be LF: with CRLF it fails on macOS and Linux with an
# unhelpful "bad interpreter" error. It is currently CRLF in this working tree, which is the one
# line-ending problem here with a real functional failure mode -- it just happens not to bite on
# Windows, so it would have gone unnoticed until someone cloned onto a Mac.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Source and config: explicit, so nothing depends on git's content sniffing.
*.java       text eol=lf
*.gradle     text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.xml        text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.txt        text eol=lf
*.py         text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch these. Normalising a jar corrupts it.
*.jar    binary
*.aar    binary
*.apk    binary
*.so     binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.tflite binary
*.zip    binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks    binary
